  </figure>
  <p>
    <xref ref="figure-chi-squared-histogram"/> is a histogram of a
    bunch of randomizations of this data and their <m>X^2</m> scores.
    This is assuming the null hypothesis: that there is no difference
    between groups. <m>X^2 = 0</m> would recover this perfect
    balance, but that basically never happens. Instead, almost all of
    the samples have some variables, but most have a low <m>X^2</m>.
    The random samples are grouped near 0 and they decay away.  Our
    sample statistic had a <m>X^2</m> of 40, which is way, way out
    compared to these randomizations. Likely, this means that something
    significant is happening: these randomizations don't seem to have
    any chance at all of producing a <m>X^2</m> of 40. 
  </p>
  <p>
    That would be doing the randomizations by hand. I don't want to do
    that. I want to make use of the fact that many clever
    mathematicians have build probability models so that I can just
    calculate directly. Happily, this is true, but the models are now
    different. This is no longer a normal distribution at all. The
    previous histogram didn't look normal at all: it started with lots
    of data and then decayed away only in the positive direction.
    There were no negatives at all (indeed, <m>X^2</m> is always a
    positive number).
  </p>
  <p>
    The theory here, then, it not based on normal distributions. It is
    based on another set of mathematical functions, unsurprisingly
    called <m>X^2</m> distributions. A normal distributions
    is entirely determined by two parameters: mean and standard
    deviations. For <m>X^2</m> distributions, there is one parameter.
    This parameter is misleadingly called <term>degrees of
    freedom</term>. <xref ref="figure-eight-chi-squared"/> shows the
    graphs of the <m>X^2</m>-distribution where the degrees of freedom
    parameter ranges from <m>1</m> to <m>8</m>. All of these
    distribution start at a high value at zero and then decay away in
    the positive direction. They don't exist at all below zero. 
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="figure-eight-chi-squared">
    <caption>Caption</caption>
    <image width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[domain=.001:15,samples=200,thick]
          \clip (-0.2,-0.2) rectangle (20,7);
          \foreach[count=\k,evaluate={\z=\k>2?"(0,0)--":"";\c=10*\k}] 
            \g in {sqrt(pi),1,sqrt(pi)/2,1,3/4*sqrt(pi),2,15/8*sqrt(pi),6}
              \draw[color=blue!\c!red,yscale=15] \z 
                plot (\x,{exp(ln(\x/2)*\k/2-ln(\x)-\x/2-ln(\g))});
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Like with normal distributions, understanding these mathematical
    functions allows us to do the hypothesis test directly, without
    having to do the randomizations ourselves. The central limit
    theorem said that the sampling distribution for a difference of
    proprotions (or a single proportion) is normal under
    certain reasonable conditions. The same is true here: there are
    conditions for the data to fit these distributions. I'm not going
    to get into the details of those conditions<mdash/>a well designed
    study with good data should satisfy, but you should be aware that
    these conditions do exist. 
  </p>
  <p>
    To use the theory, I need to know how to determine the degrees of
    freedom parameter. We are dealing with a categorical variables
    with multiple categories. This is actually pretty straightforward:
    the number of categories determines the degrees of freedom.
    Specifically, take the two cateogrical variables we have here.
    Take the number of categories in the first varible, subtract one,
    then multiply by the number of categories in the second variable,
    again subtrating one. If <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> are the number
    of categories in the two variables, then the formula is this.
    <md>
      DF = (c_1 - 1)(c_2 - 1)
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    In our cases, there were three categories of questions, so the
    first calculation here is <m>3-1</m>, and two categories of
    outcome, so <m>2-1</m> in the second calculation. The degree of
    freedom we need for this particular <m>X^2</m> process is
    <m>(3-1)(2-1) = (2)(1) = 2</m>. I will compare with a <m>X^2</m>
    distribution with two degrees of freedom. 
  </p>
  <p>
    So now I have a sample statistics and a distribution. The standard
    deviation is not as relevant for the <m>X^2</m> distribution. (It
    can, of course, be calculated, but it is not quite as descriptive
    of the data as it was for the normal distribution.) So we go
    straight to the calculation of the <m>p</m>-value of the sample
    statistic. This is the same idea: the <m>p</m>-value will be the
    area under the curve past the point of the sample statistic, and
    it will measure the probability of producing the sample statistic
    under the null hypothesis. It can be used to determine
    significance exactly as before. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, for <m>X^2</m> distribution, there is no worry about
    one-sided or two-sided tests. There is only ever a one-sided
    testo of the right tail. The <m>X^2</m> distrbution starts at 0
    and only proceeds in the positive direction. Points further out
    in the positive direction have greater variance. I only ever need
    the area of the right tail. 
  </p>
</section>
